[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"839","stub":{"id":"839","section":"Snowmobile bridge to Moxie Falls","altname":"","river":"Moxie Stream","difficulty":"V","states":["USA-MAI"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"rapid","description":"1\\. WebbÂs Dead River Campground & Shuttle Service: Shuttles to the Dead put in, campground with showers. To book a site at Webb's campground or shuttle call [River Drivers](http://www.riverdrivers.com/) to make the reservations. That phone number is: 207-663-4475. The cost is $8.00 per person, per night, plus 7% State tax.\n\n2\\. AppletonÂs restaurant: Pizza, subs, ice cream, breakfast (207) 663-2114 .\n\n3\\. [River Drivers](http://www.riverdrivers.com/): Rafting, etc 207-663-4475.\n\n4\\. Paddling shop: Dead River Outfitters\n\n5\\. BerryÂs General Store: Gas, Beer, food, supplies, pizza, subs\n\n6\\. The Ball field campground: Primitive campsites, porta-poties, no showers. Take out for Lower Kennebec.\n\n7\\. [Crab Apple](http://www.crabappleinc.com/maine_adventures.htm) Camping Rafting, hot tub, bar, food.\n\n8\\. Public Picnic area: also used as Lower Kennebec take out.\n\n9\\. The Marshall Hotel (Hotel Cocktails): Food, bar, horse shoes, pool table.\n\n10\\. Dead River takeout.\n\n11\\. [Majic Falls Rafting](http://www.magicfalls.com/): Raft trips, camping\n\n12\\. [Three Rivers Whitewater & Kelly Brook campground](http://www.threeriverswhitewater.com/)\n\n13\\. [Northern Outdoors](http://www.northernoutdoors.com/) Camping, Rafting, restaurant, hot tub, bar, entertainment\n\n14\\. [North Country Rivers](http://www.ncrivers.com/) Rafting, etc.\n\n15\\. [Professional River Runners:](http://www.proriverrunners.com/aaa/kennebec-river.htm) Rafting, etc.\n\n16\\. [New England Outdoor Center:](http://www.neoc.com/) Rafting, paddling shop, etc.\n\n17\\. Indian Pond campground\n\n**Not on map:**\n\n[Moxie Gore cabins](http://cmoxiegore.com/): : $30 per night\n\n[Moxie Outdoor Outfitters](http://www.wild-rivers.com/): 866-663-2646 Large cabins $75 per night","id":"487","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Map Key","distance":"0","difficulty":null,"approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"479","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Map of the Forks Maine","distance":"0","photoID":"2042","difficulty":null,"approximate":false},{"type":"waterfall","description":null,"id":"1152","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"JR. Falls","distance":"0.75","difficulty":"V+","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":null,"id":"1153","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Slip & Slide","distance":"1.1","difficulty":"IV+","approximate":false,"photoID":"4984"},{"type":"waterfall","description":"Two big drops, upper and lower. The lower drop has a rather meaty hole that needs to be punched. Be carefull the takeout is just below lower Double Hammer, just beyond is the unrunable 80' Moxie Falls.","id":"1154","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Double Hammer","distance":"1.7","difficulty":"V","approximate":false,"photoID":"4985"},{"type":"takeout","description":"Moxie Falls is one of the higher waterfalls in Maine with estimates ranging from 60' to 90'. It is just below the takeout and is unrunnable. The entire flow lands on a flat rock at the base of the falls.","id":"1155","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Moxie Falls","distance":"1.8","photoID":"4982","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"4982","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3573","description":"Immediately after Double Hammer take out on river left just above this falls, an 80 ft drop onto a flat rock.\r\nPS don't swim Double Hammer","caption":"Moxie Falls","poi_name":"Moxie Falls","subject":"","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":"479","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4982","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4982"}},{"id":"885504","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"888300","description":"The first rapid in the section just above Moxie Falls","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"Andrew Althage","author":"Andrew Althage","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885504","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885504"}},{"id":"885503","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"888299","description":"Just off the road that heads to the Kennebec. Underneath the metal bridge. ","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"Andrew Althage","author":"John Huisjen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885503","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885503"}},{"id":"4985","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3573","description":"","caption":"Double Hammer","poi_name":"Upper Double Hammer","subject":"Mike Rock","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":"479","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4985","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4985"}},{"id":"4984","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3573","description":"","caption":"Slip & Slide","poi_name":"Slip & Slide","subject":"Mike Rock","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":"1153","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4984","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4984"}},{"id":"4981","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3573","description":"","caption":"Lower Double Hammer on Moxie","poi_name":"Double Hammer","subject":"Beau Bannerman","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":"1154","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4981","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4981"}},{"id":"4986","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3573","description":"Looking upstream just above Moxie Falls.  Take out at the pool in the forground just below Double Hammer.","caption":"Moxie Stream","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4986","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4986"}},{"id":"7827","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3280","description":"A nice shot of the gradient above Moxie Falls","caption":"Moxie upstream of Moxie Falls","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Mark Stover","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7827","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7827"}},{"id":"4983","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3573","description":"","caption":"Long boat creekin","poi_name":"","subject":"Mike Rock","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4983","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4983"}},{"id":"890777","ext":"jpg","post_id":"894715","description":null,"caption":"John Huisjen","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Drew Althage","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/890777","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/890777"}},{"id":"890778","ext":"jpg","post_id":"894716","description":null,"caption":"Drew Althage","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/890778","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/890778"}}],"detail":{"id":"839","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Source: Greg and Sue Hanlon's [Steep Creeks of New England](http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1889787051/americanwhite-20), which has more info on this run.\n\n**Directions:** On Rte. 201, head South from Berry's General Store in The Forks about 200 yards and cross the Kennebec River. Bang the first left and head 3.3 miles to a small dirt road on the left. Turn left and follow this road 'til you come to a clearing on the left. Park here and carry down the road 0.2 miles to a washed-out snowmobile bridge which crosses Moxie Stream.\n\nTo takeout: head back to the paved road and turn right. Drive 1.2 miles to a better dirt road on the right. Follow this road until the Moxie Falls trail crosses the road and park here. Take the trail on the right about 0.3 miles to the takeout.\n\n**Be sure to take out above 80-ft Moxie Falls!** Check out the takeout before running the river, just to be sure. You don' wanna be takin' on that BadBoy, I figure.\n\n### Technical info\n\n```\nPut in elevation........924'\nTake out elevation......720'\nTotal drop..............204'\nAverage drop/mile.......116'\nDistance................1.75  miles\nRiver width average.....30'\nRiver geology...........Slate ledge\nRiver water quality.....Good, clarity good.\nScenery.................Excellent, isolated wilderness river\nWildlife................Deer, moose, osprey, eagles.\n\n```\n\n**Posted by Ran Currier on 7/6/05**\n\nif you noticed the release of one sluice\nslightly open, it is barely enough to paddle. If desperate for\ncreeking you are better off carrying your boat to the takeout and\nhiking up the last 1/3 mile to hit the good drops, the beginning is\ntoo bony.","averageGradient":"116","maxGradient":null,"length":"1.8","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["487","479","1152","1153","1154","1155"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-69.91160746859015,45.360762729591045],[-69.91163340192344,45.36076252959106],[-69.9116590685901,45.360771329591046],[-69.91171100192332,45.36077092959108],[-69.91176306858995,45.36078399625768],[-69.91180160192323,45.36079719625769],[-69.91183126858982,4